---
export const updateMeta = {
  date: '2026-08-26',
  title: 'The waitlist application becomes a three-step form on the site palette',
  tags: ['pricing', 'waitlist', 'conversion', 'design'],
};
---
<article class="border-l-4 border-[hsl(24,95%,53%)] pl-6">
  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-1">August 26, 2026</h2>
  <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-4">
    Reworked the founding-member application at
    <a href="/pricing" class="text-[hsl(24,95%,53%)] hover:underline">/pricing</a>.
    The qualification questions added earlier this month had turned it into one
    long column, and the page was rendering in a cream and amber palette that
    looked nothing like the site linking to it.
  </p>

  <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 mt-4 mb-1">Three steps instead of one column</h3>
  <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 mb-3">
    Step 1 asks who you are (name, email, kennel, species). Step 2 asks about the
    program (volume, where animals go, where records live today). Step 3 covers
    the website, what prompted the search, how they heard about us, and a phone
    number. A dot tracker shows progress; forward validates the visible step and
    back is always available, so nothing typed is lost by reviewing it.
  </p>
  <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 mb-3">
    Progressive enhancement, verified rather than assumed: the steps ship
    visible and the script hides them on first run, so with JavaScript
    unavailable the form renders as one column with every field reachable and
    still submits. The Continue button is the reverse — hidden in the markup and
    revealed by the script, because a paging control that cannot page is worse
    than no control at all.
  </p>

  <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 mt-4 mb-1">Now on the site's palette</h3>
  <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 mb-3">
    This page skips BaseLayout deliberately, to carry no nav or footer that would
    compete with the one decision. It had silently skipped
    <code class="text-xs bg-gray-100 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">bhq-dark-shell</code>
    along with it, which is the class that maps the shared utility classes onto
    the dark navy ground. The class is now applied directly, and the accent
    switched from Tailwind amber to the site's own
    <code class="text-xs bg-gray-100 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">--color-accent</code>,
    which is a visibly different hue against navy.
  </p>

  <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 mt-4 mb-1">Verified in a browser</h3>
  <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 mb-3">
    Driven in Chrome against the built page, with JavaScript on and off: step
    visibility, refusal of an incomplete step, the two multi-selects that have no
    native required state, back preserving what was typed, and the ground
    computing to the site's own background colour.
  </p>
  <p class="text-sm text-gray-600">
    Those runs caught what a class list cannot show. The shared stylesheet
    flattens every
    <code class="text-xs bg-gray-100 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">bg-gradient-to-b</code>
    under the dark shell, so both buttons rendered as invisible dark rectangles.
    The native checkboxes rendered white and read as pre-checked. Keyboard focus
    stayed on Continue after advancing, so the next Tab left the form instead of
    entering the step just revealed, and Enter on Back advanced instead of going
    back. The required-field asterisk measured 2.6:1 against the new dark ground,
    below the 4.5:1 bar; it now measures 5.2:1.
  </p>
</article>
